Master PRD: GTM Intelligence OS
Product name: GTM Intelligence OS
Document type: Product Requirements Document
Version: 1.0
Target: Portfolio-grade MVP evolving into a production SaaS
Primary users: Founders, freelancers, agencies, sales teams, and small businesses
Core idea: A multi-tenant, multi-agent platform that captures leads, understands buying intent, automates sales workflows, updates CRM records, and involves humans when decisions require approval.

1. Product Overview
1.1 Problem
Small businesses and agencies receive leads from Instagram, websites, email, WhatsApp, forms, and advertisements. Their customer data is usually fragmented, follow-ups are manual, CRM records become outdated, and salespeople lack context about which leads deserve attention.

Existing tools often solve only one part of the process:

A CRM stores customer information but does not actively execute workflows.

A chatbot answers messages but does not understand the entire sales pipeline.

Marketing tools generate leads but do not reliably qualify or follow them up.

Automation platforms connect tools but often lack business reasoning, approvals, and explainability.

The product will combine CRM, GTM operations, event-driven workflows, and specialized AI agents into one coordinated revenue system.

The platform should prioritize unified data, intent signals, CRM-ready enrichment, and orchestration because automating poor-quality data can simply amplify incorrect decisions.

1.2 Product vision
Build a reliable AI-powered GTM operating system that helps a business move from:

text
Lead arrives
→ Lead is understood
→ Lead is qualified
→ Lead receives the right response
→ Meeting or payment is completed
→ CRM stays updated
→ Revenue outcome is measured
1.3 Portfolio objective
The project should demonstrate more than chatbot development. It should prove capability in:

Multi-agent architecture.

Event-driven systems.

Durable workflow execution.

Multi-tenancy.

OAuth and third-party integrations.

CRM and data modeling.

Human-in-the-loop design.

AI evaluation.

Observability and auditability.

Secure tool execution.

Revenue analytics.

Production-focused engineering.

Every sensitive action should be attributable to a tenant, user, agent, task, tool, policy decision, approval state, and outcome.

2. Goals and Non-Goals
2.1 Goals
The platform must:

Capture leads from multiple channels.

Resolve duplicate contacts across channels.

Enrich leads and accounts with useful business information.

Score leads using transparent rules and AI analysis.

Route work to specialized agents.

Generate personalized responses and outreach.

Schedule follow-ups for inactive conversations.

Support calendar booking.

Support payment initiation and payment confirmation.

Keep CRM records synchronized.

Provide human approval for risky actions.

Provide complete agent execution traces.

Evaluate agent performance using test cases.

Support tenant-specific agents, offers, rules, prompts, and integrations.

Report funnel, pipeline, cost, and revenue metrics.

2.2 Non-goals for the first release
The initial release will not attempt to:

Replace a full enterprise CRM.

Send unlimited autonomous outbound messages.

Guarantee conversion or revenue.

Scrape websites or social networks in violation of provider policies.

Process sensitive financial or health information without proper controls.

Build a general-purpose autonomous agent platform for every industry.

Support every social, email, calendar, and payment provider at launch.

Allow agents to execute unrestricted code.

